  "query_preview": "..\n\n- Meteorological Overview: \n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will persist over the region with an upper-\nlevel trough positioned over the west coast. This will bring daily \nchances for afternoon showers and thunderstorms through Saturday, \nprimarily along the Continental Divide. Through the weekend into \nearly next week, the trough nudges a little to the east with more \ndirectly moisture flow over the region combined with a shortwave \ntrough ejecting out on Monday providing a chance for widespread \nprecipitation to the region. Temperatures will cool through early \nnext week before warming back up slightly in late next week as upper-\nlevel ridging tries to redevelop in the wake of the trough.\n\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nRain Sunday through Tuesday:\n\nThe Hi-Line still has the highest confidence for higher rainfall \namounts with Southwest Montana still favored for lower rainfall \namounts. The main area of uncertainty is the portion of Central \nMontana south of line between Great Falls and Lewistown and north of \nTownsend where the models are flip-flopping on whether or not these \nlocations will get some of the heavier precipitation or not. For the \ntime being there is 70% confidence in at least 0.50\" rainfall along \nthe northern half of our region with a 40% probability for at least \n1.0\" of rain. For the southern half of our region there is a 60% \nchance for at least 0.10\" of rain and only a 30% for greater than \n0.50\" of rain over the 2 day period. It is worth mentioning these \nare broad probabilities for the region and localized heavier rain is \npossible for both the northern and southern portions of the region.\n-thor",
